ckly those fires are spreading um  five times the acreage of california has  burnt this year is at the same stage  last year and last year was the record  when they got to the end of the year so  that shows you what's going on  this extreme fire behavior they normally  see in the autumn  is happening in the early and middle  part of the summer and the reason is  pretty clear  two very dry winters there's an extreme  drought across  this part of california you can see here  in the angeles national forest  just how dry the vegetation is here and  that is a real problem and of course  there's a heat wave on top of that  drying this vegetation out even more and  providing  fuel for these fires to spread so  quickly there's also this issue of air  quality  uh the smoke that's causing problems not  surprisingly around these these fire  areas  also spreading across the country two  thousand miles away on the east coast  they're seeing the effects  of the smoke from these fires and that  is a health concern of course  for millions of people the firefighters  doing a remarkable job we have to say  that they have saved a lot of lives this  year in what they're doing  but they know more of these fires are on  their way greg thanks very much indeed  for joining us and over the weekend  there will be a special edition of the  daily climate show we'll be looking back  at the last week which has seen  extreme weather around the world prompt  yet more warnings of climate change  and on climate cast this week we explore  how we can adapt to a warming climate  you can find that wherever you normally  get your podcasts  in today's other climate news thousands  of people have been rescued after  becoming trapped by flood waters in west  india  landslides triggered by heavy monsoon  rains have killed at least five people  in maharashtra  and left hundreds stranded on rooftops  at least 30 people died after heavy  rainfall in mumbai last week  and scientists warn changing monsoon  patter